WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.480
- [Narrator] The coronavirus
pandemic has shut down

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.080
movie theaters across the country.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.850
With people bound to their homes,

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.950
they're streaming more than ever,

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.700
and some Hollywood studios
are releasing some films

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.080
direct to streaming services
and digital platforms,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.710
shaking up a distribution model

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.680
that's been in place for decades.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.150
- The theaters are saying
you're taking advantage

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.070
of a crisis here, and the
studios are saying no,

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.080
we're meeting consumers where they are,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.210
and a lot of them are stuck at home.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.800
- [Narrator] This trend away from theaters

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.700
and toward digital
platforms began years ago,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.690
but experts say the pandemic
could accelerate that shift.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.523
Here's how.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.090
This is the path a film typically follows

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.280
as it's distributed in the US.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.120
The top big-five studios
own the legal rights

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.840
to movies they produce,
and they distribute them

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.580
to several destinations.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.670
Say a film is made and
distributed by Sony.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.990
Normally, the distributing
studio makes deals

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.100
with movie theaters like AMC to organize

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.360
a wide theatrical release
to over 3,000 screens.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.660
Theaters traditionally
have exclusive rights

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.830
to show the film for 75 to 90 days,

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.010
a period known as the
theatrical release window.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.860
After that window ends,
the film usually goes

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.070
to digital platforms like Apple or Amazon

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.320
where it might be available
for digital rental or purchase.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.610
Later, the film is distributed
again at a lower price

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.680
and may become available
on DVD or Blu Ray.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.310
Finally, the studio can
sign a deal with Netflix

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.720
or another third-party streaming service

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.133
or head to cable channels like HBO,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.470
but the coronavirus crisis
is shaking up that model.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.550
Take "Trolls World Tour."
- Yes, Papa Fur?

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.600
- [Narrator] On April 10th,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.160
Universal Pictures
digitally released the film

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.440
straight to platforms
like AppleTV for 19.99

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.580
for a 48-hour digital rental,

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.493
bypassing the theatrical opening.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.130
- So theaters have viewed
this as a philosophical

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.800
and an existential threat for many years.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.430
They wanna safeguard
that exclusivity window

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:13.880
because they know that it is
still one of the main reasons

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.420
people go to the theater,
right, is because they're

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.250
showing movies that you
cannot see anywhere else,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.880
and the experimentation
over the last several weeks

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.770
with the theaters closed
has really been a source

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.450
of friction between
studios and exhibitors.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.550
- [Narrator] The move was
a gamble, but it paid off.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.767
In just three weeks,

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:38.340
"Trolls World Tour" made
nearly $100 million in rentals,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.610
the biggest opening day and weekend

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.413
for a digital title ever.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.050
NBC-Universal CEO Jeff Shell
told the "Wall Street Journal"

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.050
that the results for "Trolls
World Tour" have exceeded

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.970
our expectations and
demonstrated the viability

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.300
of PVOD, or premium video on demand.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.190
Theaters were less enthusiastic.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.720
- Whenever Universal
announced that they were

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.520
going to do it with other
movies and planning then,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.910
this is key, planning to release movies

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.660
with some more fluidity between theaters

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.660
and digital platforms even
after theaters re-opened,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.200
it really became open warfare.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.000
- [Narrator] AMC CEO Adam
Aron sent an open letter

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.510
to Universal saying
that his theater chain,

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.460
the largest in the world,
would refuse to show

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.500
any Universal films going forward.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.970
- Universal's decision to
send "Trolls World Tour"

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.080
straight to the digital platforms
is going to be remembered

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.330
as a turning point in
the relationship between

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.780
studios and exhibitors
because it's going to force

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.810
some very hard conversations
when the theaters re-open

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:48.870
about why that might not
be a viable alternative

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:53.143
to the exclusive theatrical
distribution model.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.540
- [Narrator] This all happening

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.963
at a very bad time for theaters.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:02.310
Analysts predict there will
be zero box office revenue

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.930
in May and a 30% drop for
domestic ticket sales this year.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.560
Some are predicting that
domestic box office earnings

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.690
will be around 6.7 billion for the year

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.970
down from more than 11 billion for each

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.660
of the past five years putting
2020 on track to become

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:23.050
the worst year for the
film industry since 1997.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.440
- A lot of analysts
expect a lot of smaller,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.590
independent theaters to
really struggle through this

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.220
and potentially have to close.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.610
It remains to be seen though,
once theaters re-open,

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.140
how many screens come back online.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.100
- [Narrator] At the same time,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.550
streaming services are
seeing a rise in viewership.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.810
In April, Netflix reported
15.8 million new subscribers

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.810
for the first quarter of the year,

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.583
more than double their
forecast from the year before.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.620
- Now the big question
is what is temporary

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.270
and what is permanent?

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.320
Do consumers say hey look,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.740
why was I going out to
the movie theater at all

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.500
when I could get all this right here,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.570
or will they be so desperate to go out

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:08.890
and get outside of the house,
get outside of the living room

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.370
that they return to movie theaters

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:12.910
with even more vigor than before?

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:14.650
That's a real, looming question.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.097
- [Narrator] One thing
that's clear is that

00:05:16.097 --> 00:05:18.370
"Trolls World Tour" isn't the only movie

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.050
being released straight
to streaming right now.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.200
While big-budget tentpole
movies like "Mulan"

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.490
will still have a delayed
wide theatrical release,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.270
studios are releasing more
than a dozen smaller movies

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.470
direct to digital because of the pandemic.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.150
In April, The Academy
announced that movies released

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.200
via streaming would be eligible
for the upcoming Oscars

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.960
just this year because of coronavirus.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.670
- A, we don't know how
comfortable people might be

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:48.020
going back to the theater,
and B, we don't know how long

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.720
that might take to get them there,

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.290
but the other things that's happened

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:54.080
is that because so many of the big movies,

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.470
the James Bond movies, the Marvel movies,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.680
have been pushed to next year,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.420
many analysts are saying we
might see some cannibalization

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.710
at the box office next year, too.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.580
- [Narrator] Experts
say that the box office

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.550
isn't likely to disappear forever.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.110
- One lesson from the
pandemic will be that studios

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:16.470
have been emboldened to experiment

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.773
with these distribution strategies
more than they ever were.

00:06:20.663 --> 00:06:23.413
(pleasant music)

